      "math_academy_id": "e-1608",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/51bf9631f8b2ca90bda343a84a675f302ebae205446e528f5f05a8ea1f5f577b.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14156876/assets/f3edb95bc0a0a57a6dc439a06e1653d95c23dcd49e701b4d4d3f3d8c0f36c594.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{y+1}.$",
